"""
Flag-gated Ref emission for class-typed call arguments (lane SF1,
2026-07-28) — SIMPLE_BORROW_REF_CALL_ARGS.

`_MirLoweringExpr/switch_operators_calls.spl` (around line 3028) records a
class-typed call argument as a Mutable MIR `Ref` (feeding the NLL borrow
checker) only when `SIMPLE_BORROW_REF_CALL_ARGS=1`. Default-off, because
s19 semantics make every class parameter mutable-by-design
(write-through), so emitting honest Mutable refs unconditionally would
flag ordinary valid aliasing (passing the same object to two calls) as a
borrow conflict.

## Why this is a source-shape spec, not an executed compile

An executed-pipeline version of this spec (compile a real `class Counter
+ fn bump(c: Counter) + fn main()` program through Parse -> HIR -> MIR and
count `Ref` instructions with the env var on/off) was attempted and then
DROPPED: it was non-deterministic in a way unrelated to the flag itself.
`mir.functions.keys().len()` came back as bare 0 (no functions at all, not
"functions but no Ref") on some invocations and 2 on others, for
byte-identical source, with no observable difference in inputs -- this
reproduced under both `bin/simple test` and `bin/simple run`, with and
without cache-busting identifiers/filenames, and is consistent with this
codebase's documented history of the self-hosted compiler being
unreliable when invoked *from* a running interpreted Simple program (see
`.claude/memory/ref_*` engine-parity notes). A flaky spec is worse than no
spec, so this asserts the STATIC shape of the guard instead: the
condition string, the env var name, the default-off `?? ""`, and that the
`emit_ref(..., MirBorrowKind.Mutable, ...)` call is textually inside that
guarded block. This is a regression tripwire for the flag being
accidentally deleted, renamed, or defaulted on -- not a substitute for
execution evidence.

## Manual verification (execution evidence, captured directly, not run by this spec)

Standalone script compiling `class Counter: n: i64 / fn bump(c: Counter):
c.n = c.n + 1 / fn main() -> i64: var counter = Counter(n: 0); bump(counter);
counter.n` through the same Parse -> HIR -> MIR pipeline, run twice as
separate `bin/simple run` processes (2026-07-28):
  - env unset: 0 `MirInstKind.Ref` instructions in the compiled module.
  - `SIMPLE_BORROW_REF_CALL_ARGS=1`: 1 `MirInstKind.Ref` instruction, in
    `main`'s block, immediately before the `bump(counter)` call.
Both runs printed `functions: 2` (bump, main) confirming a real,
non-empty compile in both cases.
"""

use std.spec

extern fn rt_file_read_text(path: text) -> text

val guard_source_path = "src/compiler/50.mir/_MirLoweringExpr/switch_operators_calls.spl"

fn read_guard_source() -> text:
    rt_file_read_text(guard_source_path)

describe "SIMPLE_BORROW_REF_CALL_ARGS flag-gated Ref emission (source-shape guard)":
    it "gates class-typed call-arg Ref emission behind the documented env var":
        val src = read_guard_source()
        expect(src.contains("SIMPLE_BORROW_REF_CALL_ARGS")).to_equal(true)

    it "defaults OFF: the check compares against the literal \"1\", with an empty-string fallback":
        val src = read_guard_source()
        expect(src.contains("(rt_env_get(\"SIMPLE_BORROW_REF_CALL_ARGS\") ?? \"\") == \"1\"")).to_equal(true)

    it "the Mutable Ref emission is textually inside the flag guard, not unconditional":
        val src = read_guard_source()
        val guard_idx = src.index_of("(rt_env_get(\"SIMPLE_BORROW_REF_CALL_ARGS\") ?? \"\") == \"1\"") ?? -1
        val emit_idx = src.index_of("emit_ref(MirPlace.local_place(arg_local), MirBorrowKind.Mutable") ?? -1
        expect(guard_idx >= 0).to_equal(true)
        expect(emit_idx >= 0).to_equal(true)
        expect(emit_idx > guard_idx).to_equal(true)
        # And close enough to be the SAME if-block, not some unrelated
        # later use of emit_ref elsewhere in a 3000+ line file.
        expect(emit_idx - guard_idx < 1000).to_equal(true)
